1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,995
A course planner, inside ChatGPT's browser.

2
00:00:01,995 --> 00:00:05,654
It shows what a student
picked — all any timetable shows.

3
00:00:05,654 --> 00:00:10,975
Not what it cost them two years later, when
a specialisation they wanted is quietly gone.

4
00:00:12,475 --> 00:00:14,447
I fill in my first year by hand.

5
00:00:14,447 --> 00:00:21,350
Each click is a tool call — the same tools an agent would use,
tagged page, because a page can only be sure about calls it makes itself.

6
00:00:24,850 --> 00:00:28,475
One slot, two courses that fit
it. So I ask the page to weigh them.

7
00:00:33,475 --> 00:00:35,707
Numerical Methods closes
Graphics and Animation.

8
00:00:35,707 --> 00:00:37,939
Geometry closes Data and Machine Learning.

9
00:00:37,939 --> 00:00:42,404
Neither is free — what costs
is the slot, not the course.

10
00:00:42,404 --> 00:00:49,100
That is reachability over a prerequisite graph
under a credit budget, and nobody does it in their head.

11
00:00:52,600 --> 00:00:57,280
Now the part that runs the other way.
Every rule so far belongs to the university.

12
00:00:57,280 --> 00:00:58,450
This one is mine.

13
00:01:02,950 --> 00:01:05,725
So I ask for the course
that would close it anyway.

14
00:01:10,725 --> 00:01:14,000
Refused — and it quotes me
back to myself, not the handbook.

15
00:01:14,000 --> 00:01:20,550
It offers the way out and prices it: I can lift my own
limit, but the specialisation goes, and it does not come back.

16
00:01:23,550 --> 00:01:25,984
And that part is not really about courses.

17
00:01:25,984 --> 00:01:32,678
Anyone handing work to an agent has the same question,
and the usual answer is a prompt — which is a request.

18
00:01:32,678 --> 00:01:36,025
This is a limit that outlives
the conversation that set it.

19
00:01:38,025 --> 00:01:46,055
All of that is on a timeline and any of it rewinds —
including the limit, because a limit is an event like any other.

20
00:01:46,055 --> 00:01:48,625
Undo is the same reducer over fewer events.

21
00:01:50,625 --> 00:01:53,922
Thirteen tools, registered with
document dot modelContext dot registerTool.

22
00:01:53,922 --> 00:01:58,318
What shaped all of it: execute
returns a string the model reads.

23
00:01:58,318 --> 00:02:00,150
These strings are the interface.

24
00:02:02,150 --> 00:02:09,375
So a tool that changes something returns the resulting plan, never
'ok' — or the agent's picture of the state drifts from the page's.

25
00:02:11,375 --> 00:02:11,991
Last thing.

26
00:02:11,991 --> 00:02:19,687
WebMCP gives a page no way to know who called it, so this
page counts calls by origin and says the attribution is a guess.

27
00:02:19,687 --> 00:02:25,536
That caught an assistant answering about this page
correctly and in detail — having called no tool at all.

28
00:02:25,536 --> 00:02:27,075
It had read the repository.
